Sure, I'd be happy to help you create an async/await function for asynchronous operations. Here's an example of a function that fetches data from a remote API using the `fetch` API, which is an asynchronous operation. This function will return a promise that resolves to the data when the API request is completed.

```javascript
// async/await function to fetch data from a remote API
async function fetchData() {
  // Replace 'https://api.example.com/data' with the actual API endpoint
 const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');

  // Check if the request was successful
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);
  }

  // Parse the JSON response
 const data = await response.json();

  // Return the data
  return data;
}

// Example usage
fetchData()
  .then(data => {
    console.log('Asynchronous result:', data);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error('Error:', error);
  });
```

This function demonstrates how to use async/await to perform asynchronous operations, and how to handle errors and resolve promises. You can replace the API endpoint with the actual URL of the API you want to fetch data from.
